          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Predicting What Comes Next: Exploring Sequences and Progressions"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.844,
            0.068,
            0.884,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "187"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.108,
            0.444,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "These may be rewritten as"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.246,
            0.13,
            0.785,
            0.15
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_3.jpg",
          "content": "3 , 3 \\times 2 , 3 \\times 4 , 3 \\times 8 , \\ldots \\mathrm { o r } \\; \\mathrm { a s } \\; 3 , 3 \\times 2 , 3 \\times 2 ^ { \\mathrm { \\textell } _ { \\mathrm { \\textell } } } , 3 \\times 2 ^ { \\mathrm { \\textell } _ { \\mathrm { \\textell } } } , \\ldots"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.152,
            0.836,
            0.173
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "If we treat these expressions as the terms of a sequence, we get"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.328,
            0.174,
            0.707,
            0.196
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_5.jpg",
          "content": "t_{1} = 3, t_{2} = 3 \\times 2, t_{3} = 3 \\times 2^{2}, t_{4} = 3 \\times 2^{3}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.196,
            0.883,
            0.238
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "and so on. We can write the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term as "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { \\mathrm { n } } = 3 \\times 2 ^ { \\mathrm { n - 1 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . As a recursive formula, we can write "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { 1 } = 3"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { \\mathrm { n } } = 2 t _ { \\mathrm { n - 1 } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n \\geq 2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.238,
            0.886,
            0.334
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The first six terms of the sequence are 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96. Each term of the sequence is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 2. This "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "constant multiplier",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is also known as the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "common ratio",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of the sequence. We see that the ratios of consecutive pairs of terms are all the same."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.379,
            0.34,
            0.658,
            0.376
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{6}{3} = \\frac{12}{6} = \\frac{24}{12} = \\frac{48}{24} = \\frac{96}{48} = 2."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.38,
            0.886,
            0.459
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Such a sequence with a common ratio is known as a "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Geometric Progression or GP.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " In general, a GP may be described in the form of a sequence as a, ar, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "a r ^ { 2 } ,   a r ^ { 3 } ,   \\ldots ,   a r ^ { \\scriptstyle { \\mathrm { n - 1 } } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "^ { \\circ } a ^ { \\prime }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the first term, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\boldsymbol { ^ { * } r ^ { * } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "common ratio",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t _ { _ { n } } = a r ^ { _ { \\mathrm { n - 1 } } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is the "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\boldsymbol{n}^{th}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "term",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.46,
            0.885,
            0.499
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 6:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Is 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, … a geometric progression? If so, what is the common ratio?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.501,
            0.886,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 7:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Is 1, 3, 9, 27, 81, … a geometric progression? If so, what is the common ratio?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.543,
            0.886,
            0.585
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 8:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Is 1, –1, 1, –1, 1, … a geometric progression? If so, what is the common ratio?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.587,
            0.885,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 9:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Check whether the sequence 5, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\frac { 1 5 } { 4 } } ,   { \\frac { 4 5 } { 1 6 } } ,   { \\frac { 1 3 5 } { 6 4 } } , \\ldots"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a geometric progression and find its "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n ^ { t h }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " term."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.636,
            0.703,
            0.67
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In the given sequence "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "t_{1}=5, t_{2}=\\frac{15}{4}, t_{3}=\\frac{45}{16}, t_{4}=\\frac{135}{64}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.678,
            0.748,
            0.699
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us evaluate the ratios of consecutive pairs of terms."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.397,
            0.705,
            0.641,
            0.751
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_16.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{t_{2}}{t_{1}}=\\frac{15}{4} \\div 5=\\frac{15}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{5}=\\frac{3}{4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.397,
            0.76,
            0.673,
            0.807
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_17.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{t_{3}}{t_{2}}=\\frac{45}{16}\\div\\frac{15}{4}=\\frac{45}{16}\\times\\frac{4}{15}=\\frac{3}{4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.396,
            0.814,
            0.7,
            0.861
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_13_equation_18.jpg",
          "content": "\\frac{t_{4}}{t_{3}}=\\frac{135}{64}\\div\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{135}{64}\\times\\frac{16}{45}=\\frac{3}{4}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.862,
            0.884,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ratio of consecutive pairs of terms is a constant "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\frac { 3 } { 4 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Hence the"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
